<h2 id="old-kindle-rescue-may-2026">Your Old Kindle Just Got Bricked — How to Rescue Your Ebook Library</h2>

<p>On <strong>May 20, 2026</strong>, Amazon quietly cut off all pre-2012 Kindle e-readers from purchasing books. If you own a 1st-generation Kindle Paperwhite, Kindle Touch, Kindle Keyboard, Kindle 4, Kindle DX, or any early Kindle Fire model, your device can no longer buy books from the Kindle Store.</p>

<p>Combined with Amazon's earlier removal of the <strong>"Download &amp; Transfer via USB"</strong> feature, these older devices are now effectively cut off from Amazon's ecosystem. The good news: your ebook library is not lost. This article walks you through exactly what happened, how to rescue your books, and how to migrate to an open EPUB-based library that no company can take away.</p>

<hr />

<h2 id="what-happened-may-20-2026">What Happened on May 20, 2026</h2>

<p>On May 20, 2026, Amazon's servers stopped authorizing store transactions for Kindle devices released before 2012. This means the following devices can no longer:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Purchase new books</strong> from the Kindle Store</li>
  <li><strong>Borrow titles</strong> through Kindle Unlimited or Prime Reading</li>
  <li><strong>Sync</strong> library content from the cloud over WhisperSync</li>
  <li><strong>Access</strong> the Kindle Store interface on the device itself</li>
</ul>

<p>The affected devices include:</p>

