</tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>If your device is newer than these models (Paperwhite 2nd gen or later, Kindle Voyage, Oasis, or any 10th-gen or 11th-gen Kindle), you are not affected by this change.</p>

<hr />

<h2 id="can-you-still-read-books-on-the-device">Can You Still Read Books on the Device?</h2>

<p><strong>Yes — for now.</strong> Books that are already on your old Kindle can still be read. The device still turns on, pages still turn, and the e-ink screen still works. What you lose is the ability to add new content through Amazon's ecosystem.</p>

<p>However, there are two important caveats:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Battery degradation</strong> — These devices are 14-18 years old. Even if the store were still functional, battery life has likely diminished significantly. Some users report batteries swelling or failing completely.</li>
  <li><strong>No security updates</strong> — Amazon has not issued firmware updates for these devices in years. A device that cannot connect securely to Amazon's servers is also vulnerable to other connectivity issues.</li>
</ul>

<p>This makes the May 2026 cutoff a natural end-of-life milestone. Rather than trying to keep an ancient device running on life support, the smarter move is to rescue your library and move to a modern EPUB-based reading setup.</p>

<hr />

<h2 id="step-by-step-rescue-your-kindle-library">Step by Step: Rescue Your Kindle Library to EPUB</h2>

<p>Here is how to extract every book from your old Kindle and convert it to EPUB so you can read it on any modern device.</p>

<h3>Step 1: Connect Your Kindle to a Computer</h3>
<p>Use a micro-USB (or USB-C for newer models that may have been updated) cable to connect your Kindle to a Windows, Mac, or Linux computer. The Kindle will appear as a removable drive. If it does not, try a different cable — many micro-USB cables are charge-only and lack data pins.</p>

<h3>Step 2: Locate the Documents Folder</h3>
<p>On the Kindle drive, open the <strong>documents</strong> folder. This contains all your books in Amazon's proprietary formats: <code>.azw</code>, <code>.azw3</code>, <code>.mobi</code>, and <code>.kfx</code>.</p>

<h3>Step 3: Copy All Files to Your Computer</h3>
<p>Select all files in the <strong>documents</strong> folder and copy them to a new folder on your computer called something like <code>kindle-library-backup-2026</code>. Do this before the device stops working entirely — once the battery dies or the device fails, these files may be unrecoverable.</p>

<h3>Step 4: Convert to EPUB Using converter-epub.com</h3>
<p>For books that are DRM-free, you can convert them directly using our free online tool at <strong>converter-epub.com</strong>. Upload the <code>.mobi</code> or <code>.azw3</code> file and download the EPUB version. The process takes just a few seconds and preserves your book's formatting, images, and table of contents.</p>

<p>For books with DRM protection, the files on the device may still be encrypted to your Kindle's serial number. In that case, you will need to use more advanced tools — see the FAQ section below for options.</p>

<h3>Step 5: Transfer EPUBs to Your New Device</h3>
<p>Once you have EPUB files, you can read them on virtually any modern device:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Kobo</strong> — Supports EPUB natively. Just drag and drop via USB.</li>
  <li><strong>PocketBook</strong> — Full EPUB support with excellent typography.</li>
  <li><strong>Onyx Boox</strong> — Android-based, install any EPUB reader app.</li>
  <li><strong>Phone or tablet</strong> — Use apps like Google Play Books, Apple Books, or KyBook 3.</li>
  <li><strong>New Kindle</strong> — Use Amazon's <a href="/blog/send-epub-to-kindle/">Send to Kindle</a> feature, which now accepts EPUB files directly.</li>
</ul>

<p>For a more detailed walkthrough of the full migration process, including how to handle DRM-protected books and transfer your library in bulk, see our guide: <a href="/blog/migrate-kindle-library-to-epub/">How to Migrate Your Kindle Library to EPUB</a>.</p>

<hr />

