<p>For a full comparison of the major ebook readers available in 2026, including battery life, screen quality, and ecosystem differences, see our guide: <a href="/blog/kobo-vs-kindle-vs-pocketbook-2026/">Kobo vs Kindle vs PocketBook 2026</a>.</p>

<p>Kobo is particularly worth considering in 2026 because of its new partnership with <strong>StoryGraph</strong> — the popular independent reading tracker and book recommendation platform. This integration, combined with Kobo's native EPUB support and OverDrive library integration, makes it the strongest anti-Amazon reading ecosystem available.</p>

<hr />

<h2 id="why-this-happened">Why This Happened: Amazon's Aging Hardware Strategy</h2>

<p>Amazon's decision to cut off pre-2012 Kindles is not surprising. These devices run on outdated wireless protocols (3G in many cases), use an aging version of the Kindle software framework, and have batteries that are well past their expected lifespan. Supporting store transactions, security certificates, and cloud sync for 15+ year old hardware is technically expensive and serves a shrinking user base.</p>

<p>However, the way Amazon handled this — a quiet server-side cutoff with no advance warning to affected users — has frustrated many long-time Kindle owners. Combined with the earlier removal of "Download &amp; Transfer via USB" and the <a href="/blog/kindle-for-pc-shutdown-2026/">Kindle for PC shutdown on June 30, 2026</a>, this year marks a clear shift in Amazon's approach to older hardware and library portability.</p>

<p>The broader lesson: proprietary ecosystems can change the rules at any time. EPUB is an open standard maintained by the W3C, which means no single company can unilaterally cut off access to your EPUB library.</p>

<hr />

<h2 id="how-to-prevent-this-from-happening-again">How to Prevent This From Happening Again</h2>

<p>The May 2026 Kindle bricking is a wake-up call for anyone who has been relying on Amazon's ecosystem without a backup plan. Here is a strategy that protects your library regardless of what Amazon does next:</p>

<ol>
  <li><strong>Buy EPUB-friendly devices</strong> — Choose an e-reader that supports EPUB natively (Kobo, PocketBook, Onyx Boox). EPUB is an open format; any device you buy today will work with your library.</li>
  <li><strong>Keep local backups</strong> — Store your EPUB files on your computer or a cloud drive (Google Drive, Dropbox, NAS). Do not rely solely on Amazon's cloud to host your library.</li>
  <li><strong>Use Calibre for library management</strong> — Import all your books into Calibre, organize them with tags and metadata, and use it to convert between formats when needed. For a detailed comparison of Calibre against other tools, see <a href="/blog/calibre-vs-sigil-2026/">Calibre vs Sigil 2026</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>Convert everything to EPUB</strong> — Whenever you buy or acquire a book in a proprietary format, convert it to EPUB and add it to your local library. This ensures future-proof access regardless of changes to individual platforms.</li>
  <li><strong>Prefer DRM-free retailers</strong> — When buying ebooks, check whether the publisher offers DRM-free EPUB downloads. Amazon now allows DRM-free EPUB downloads for many publishers — see our <a href="/blog/amazon-drm-free-epub-download-2026/">guide to DRM-free Kindle EPUB downloads</a> for details.</li>
</ol>

