  <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
    <div itemprop="text">
      <p>It is unlikely. Amazon has a pattern of quietly discontinuing support for older Kindle hardware. The 3G shutdown for older Kindles (1st and 2nd gen) happened years ago, and users who complained received discount codes for new devices — not restored access. A similar outcome is expected here.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
  <h3 itemprop="name">Can I jailbreak my old Kindle to bypass the restriction?</h3>
  <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
    <div itemprop="text">
      <p>Jailbreaking an old Kindle is technically possible for some models, but it requires specific firmware versions that your device may or may not be running. Even with a jailbreak, the device's outdated 3G or Wi-Fi hardware may not support modern security protocols, making any workaround unreliable. Extracting your library via USB (as described above) is a simpler and more reliable approach.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
  <h3 itemprop="name">How do I convert .azw/.mobi files to EPUB?</h3>
  <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
    <div itemprop="text">
      <p>For DRM-free files, use converter-epub.com — upload the file, choose EPUB as output, and download. For DRM-protected files, you will first need to remove the DRM using tools like Calibre with the DeDRM plugin. Our guide <a href="/blog/kindle-to-epub/">How to Convert Kindle Books to EPUB</a> covers both cases in detail.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
  <h3 itemprop="name">My old Kindle's battery is swollen. Can I still rescue the books?</h3>
  <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
    <div itemprop="text">
      <p><strong>Be careful.</strong> A swollen lithium-ion battery can be a fire hazard. Do not attempt to charge the device. If you can still connect it via USB and the computer recognizes it as a drive, copy the documents folder immediately and then dispose of the device properly (many electronics retailers accept lithium-ion batteries for recycling). If the device no longer powers on, the NAND flash storage may still be readable if you disconnect the battery and connect the mainboard to a computer — but this requires technical skill and is not recommended for casual users.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

</section>

